{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"b87bf5f5-0e04-42b8-b928-02b807935c82","status":"SUCCESS","response":"### Cause\n\nThe site is a client-side rendered SPA ([site/index.html](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/site/index.html) + [site/app.js](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/site/app.js)). When visiting `/n/\u003cslug\u003e`, CloudFront serves `/index.html` with `HTTP 200` and [site/app.js](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/site/app.js#L6-L11) fetches the note content from `/v1/notes/\u003cslug\u003e`. If that `fetch()` fails, the `catch` block ([site/app.js](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/site/app.js#L18-L20)) renders:\n\n\u003e `NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address. Back to all notes`\n\nOn **2026-09-07**, `Disallow: /v1/` was added to [site/robots.txt](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/site/robots.txt) ([api/routes.md](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/api/routes.md#L14-L16)). Unlike regular browsers or `curl`, Googlebot's Web Rendering Service enforces `robots.txt` on XHR/`fetch()` calls made during JavaScript rendering. Because `/v1/` is blocked by `robots.txt`, Googlebot blocks `fetch(\"/v1/notes/\u003cslug\u003e\")`, triggers the `catch` block, and sees `\"NOT FOUND\"` on an `HTTP 200` page—classifying every note crawled after September 7 as a **Soft 404** ([search-console/url-inspection-drip-line-pressure.txt](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/search-console/url-inspection-drip-line-pressure.txt)).\n\n### Fix\n\n1. Updated [site/robots.txt](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/site/robots.txt) to allow crawling of the public `/v1/notes` and `/v1/notes/\u003cslug\u003e` endpoints while still disallowing the rest of `/v1/` (such as `/v1/me`, `/v1/auth/token`, and `/v1/notes/*/reactions`).\n2. Documented the full root cause and fix in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-QaiKy6/ANSWER.md).\n","duration_seconds":63.906713,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":38100,"output_tokens":2373,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":62661,"total_tokens":40473}}}
